course: chemistry
lesson: 6
title: Mixing and dissolving
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Stir sugar into hot tea and it seems to vanish, yet the tea tastes sweet: the sugar has dissolved, not disappeared.
explain: Water molecules crowd round each tiny sugar molecule and carry it off into the liquid, so the sugar is still there, only spread out too small to see.
explain: The sugar plus the water make a solution; let the water evaporate and the sugar comes back.
explain: Not everything dissolves: sand just sinks, and a glass of water can only hold so much salt before the rest settles at the bottom.
explain: Heat, stirring and small grains all make dissolving faster, which is why loose sugar vanishes before a sugar cube does.
fact: When something dissolves, its particles spread out among the water particles; it is still there and still has its taste.
fact: A dissolved solid can be got back by letting the water evaporate.
fact: Hot water dissolves sugar faster and can hold more of it than cold water.
fact: Water can hold only a certain amount of salt; after that it is saturated and extra salt settles at the bottom.
fact: The Dead Sea is so salty that people float on it like corks; salty water holds you up more than fresh water.
fact: Sand does not dissolve in water, because its particles are joined too strongly for water to pull them apart; nail varnish does not dissolve in water either, but it does dissolve in nail varnish remover.
fact: Small grains dissolve faster than a lump, because they have more surface for the water to touch; stirring helps too.
fact: Squash is a solution: more water makes it more dilute, and a sieve or filter cannot take the cordial back out.
fact: Gases dissolve in water too; fish breathe oxygen dissolved in the water, taking it in through their gills, and warm water holds less of it.
fact: A bath bomb holds a dry acid and baking soda that react only when water lets them meet, making carbon dioxide bubbles.
fact: Instant coffee is coffee liquid that was dried; ground coffee does not fully dissolve, so it needs a filter.
fact: As sugar dissolves you can see wavy streams, because the sugary water bends light differently from plain water.
exercise: Sugar stirred into hot tea seems to vanish. Where is it? | Spread out in the tea as tiny molecules | Destroyed by the heat of the tea, so nothing is left of it | Sunk under the tea leaves | Turned into steam above the cup
exercise: Why does sugar dissolve faster in hot tea than in iced tea? | Hot water molecules move faster and pull the sugar away sooner | Iced tea has no water left in it, only tiny bits of frozen ice | Cold sugar is harder than warm sugar | Ice cubes eat the sugar
exercise: You keep adding salt to water and at last it piles up at the bottom. Why? | The water is saturated and cannot hold any more | The salt has turned into sand at the bottom of the water | The water has frozen around it | The glass has run out of space
exercise: What happens when you stir sand into water and stop? | It sinks, never dissolved | The sand dissolves like sugar | The sand floats on top forever | The sand turns the water to mud for good
exercise: Which dissolves faster in water, a sugar cube or loose sugar? | Loose sugar: more surface | The sugar cube, it is heavier | Both take exactly the same time | Neither, sugar does not dissolve
exercise: You add lots more water to a glass of squash. What happens? | It becomes more dilute and tastes weaker | It becomes stronger and sweeter, because there is more of it | The cordial sinks to the bottom | The cordial floats up to the top
exercise: How do fish breathe under water? | They take in oxygen dissolved in the water through their gills | They hold their breath for days | They swim up to the top for a gulp of fresh air every single minute | They breathe the hydrogen in water
exercise: Why does a bath bomb fizz only in the bath? | Its acid and baking soda need water to meet and react | It is switched on by warm air | The bath water contains special bubbles that are put there by the soap makers | The box has a lid that stops the fizz
exercise: Why does instant coffee dissolve but ground coffee does not fully? | Instant coffee is dried coffee liquid, ground coffee is bits of bean | Ground coffee is mixed with sand at the factory to make the packet heavier | Instant coffee is a gas | Ground coffee only dissolves in milk
exercise: What are the wavy streams coming off a sugar lump in water? | Sugary water bending light | Air escaping from inside the sugar lump | Tiny worms that live in sugar | Heat rising up from the sugar
exercise: How could you get the salt back from salty water? | Let the water evaporate away | Pour it through a coffee filter | Stir it very fast for an hour | Put it in the fridge overnight
exercise: Why doesn't nail varnish wash off with water? | It does not dissolve in water, only in remover | It is made of solid metal | Water makes it set harder | It soaks too deep into the nail to reach
exercise: Why might fish die in a pond during a heatwave? | Warm water holds less dissolved oxygen | The sun cooks them slowly as they swim at the surface | Warm water has more salt | Heat makes the pond water poisonous
exercise: Name the ways to make sugar dissolve faster. | Use hot water, stir, and crush the sugar | Use cold water, wait patiently, and keep the sugar in big lumps | Add salt, shake the cup, and wait | Use less water and keep still
test: Your friend says: "Sugar in tea is gone, it's just sweet water now, nothing left." What could prove her wrong? | Let the water evaporate and sugar is left behind | Taste it to check that it is not sweet any more | Pour it through a fine kitchen sieve to catch all the sugar | Hold it up to the light
test: "why can't I just sieve the sugar outta my squash, it's too sweet" Best answer? | Dissolved sugar is too small to be caught, add water instead | Sieves are only for flour | The sugar would block the holes in the sieve | You can, but only with a wooden sieve that has very small holes
test: What would happen if you used ice-cold water to make a strong syrup? | Much less sugar would dissolve, and slowly | More sugar would dissolve than in hot water | The sugar would freeze into ice | It would turn into honey
test: Why is it easier to float in the sea than in a swimming pool? | Salty water holds you up more than fresh water | Sea water is much warmer than pool water | Waves push you up all the time | Pool water has no particles in it
test: Your cousin pours ground coffee into hot water and wonders why it is gritty. What is the reason? | Ground coffee does not fully dissolve, it needs a filter | He used too little water | Ground coffee only dissolves properly in water that is ice cold | The cup was not stirred clockwise
test: A cartoon fish gasps in a pond on a scorching day and someone says "it's too sunny for its eyes". What is the real problem? | Warm water holds less oxygen for the fish to breathe | Fish are scared of sunlight and hold their breath until it goes away | The water has turned to salt | The fish has forgotten how to swim
test: "chucked a whole sugar cube in, still there after ages, dunno why" What would speed it up? | Crush it, use hotter water and stir | Use colder water and wait a lot longer | Drop in a few more sugar cubes to help it | Put a lid on the cup to keep the heat in
test: Why do sugar crystals sometimes appear at the bottom of an old jar of syrup? | It holds more sugar than it can keep dissolved as it cools | Ants carry them in at night | Syrup slowly turns into salt over time | The glass grows crystals
test: Effervescent vitamin tablets fizz in water. What do they have in common with a bath bomb? | A dry acid and soda that react once water lets them meet | They both contain soap | They both have air pumped into them at the factory so they fizz when opened | They both are made of sugar only
test: Your brother says sand dissolves if you stir long enough. Is he right? | No, water cannot pull apart sand's strongly joined particles | Yes, after about an hour of stirring | Yes, if the water is very cold and you stir it the other way round | Only on a beach
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the dissolution of sugar in water? | The sugar molecules become dispersed amongst the water molecules, remaining present | The sugar is destroyed by the water and ceases altogether to exist | The sugar is transformed into water | The sugar settles invisibly at the base of the vessel
exam: It is given that further salt, added to water, remains undissolved at the base of the vessel. Identify the term applicable to the solution. | Saturated | Very dilute | Evaporated | Neutralised
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the rate of dissolution of a sugar cube. | It is increased by heating, by stirring and by reduction of the cube to small grains | It is increased by cooling the water to near freezing and by leaving it entirely undisturbed | It is invariable whatever the conditions | It is increased by enlarging the cube
exam: Identify the principal reason for which fish in a pond may suffer during periods of excessive heat. | Warm water retains a lesser quantity of dissolved oxygen | Warm water contains a greater quantity of dissolved salt | Sunlight renders pond water poisonous | Fish are unable to swim in warm water
